---
import Layout from '../../components/Layout.astro';
import Article from '../../components/Article.astro';
import { getDict } from '../../i18n/dictionaries';
import { SITE, articleJsonLd, breadcrumbJsonLd, guideHreflang } from '../../lib/seo';
const dict = getDict('en');
const title = 'Decibel Comparison Chart';
const description = 'Approximate everyday sound levels from 30 to 120 dBA for context. Browser readings remain estimates, not medical advice.';
const url = `${SITE}/decibel-chart/`;
const rows = [
  ['30 dBA', 'Quiet bedroom at night'],
  ['40 dBA', 'Quiet library'],
  ['50 dBA', 'Moderate rainfall, quiet office'],
  ['60 dBA', 'Normal conversation'],
  ['70 dBA', 'Busy traffic at close range'],
  ['80 dBA', 'Loud restaurant, alarm clock'],
  ['90 dBA', 'Lawn mower, motorcycle'],
  ['100 dBA', 'Concert front rows'],
  ['110 dBA', 'Rock concert, power tools'],
  ['120 dBA', 'Siren at close range'],
];
const jsonLd = [
  articleJsonLd({ headline: title, description, url, inLanguage: 'en', dateModified: '2026-09-19' }),
  breadcrumbJsonLd([
    { name: 'Home', url: `${SITE}/` },
    { name: 'Decibel chart', url },
  ]),
];
---

<Layout locale="en" dict={dict} title={`${title} — Real Decibel Meter`} description={description} path="/decibel-chart/" hreflang={guideHreflang('decibel-chart')} jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Article title={title} intro="Approximate reference levels for everyday sounds. Use for context only — your browser reading is an estimate, not a verified instrument measurement." reviewed="2026-09-19" breadcrumb={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'Decibel chart', href: '/decibel-chart/' }]}>
    <table class="w-full text-sm mt-2" style="border-collapse:collapse">
      <caption class="sr-only">Approximate everyday sound levels in dBA</caption>
      <thead><tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"><th scope="col" class="text-left py-2 pr-4">Level (approx.)</th><th scope="col" class="text-left py-2">Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {rows.map((r) => (<tr style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border)"><td class="tabular py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">{r[0]}</td><td class="py-2">{r[1]}</td></tr>))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <h2 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">How to read this table</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">All examples are approximate: distance, room reflections, duration and weighting change real readings. The decibel scale is logarithmic — 10 dB more means roughly ten times the sound energy. Compare your <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/#measure">online decibel meter</a> result only after <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/calibration/">calibrating your microphone</a>, and match the weighting (usually dBA).</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm">This table is educational context, not medical or regulatory advice. No hearing-safety verdicts are shown on this site because uncalibrated browser readings cannot support them. For protection decisions consult qualified guidance such as <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/noise/about/noiseandsound.html">CDC/NIOSH</a> and a calibrated meter (<a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/accuracy/">accuracy and limitations</a>).</p>
    <h2 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">Sources and technical references</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">Example levels are intentionally approximate. For occupational measurement practice and app limitations, consult <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/noise/about/app.html">CDC/NIOSH Sound Level Meter guidance</a>.</p>
    <h2 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">Match your room to the chart in three steps</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal ml-5 space-y-2 mt-2">
      <li>Calibrate with the same A-weighting you plan to compare with, keeping the phone, case and input unchanged.</li>
      <li>Set the device down and average a full 30 to 60 seconds on Slow response, noting Leq rather than the liveliest peak.</li>
      <li>Round away decimals and place the Leq beside the nearest chart row. A steady 41 belongs with the quiet-library row; a 66 with voices belongs beside conversation, not beside traffic.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-2">Expect wobble: model spreads of 5 to 10 dB between phones are ordinary, readings below about 30 dBA run high on many handsets, levels near 95 to 100 dBA may clip, and thin bass response understates rumble. Treat the chart as neighbourhood context for a screening check, never as a verdict.</p>
    <h2 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">Why the same sofa reads 42 one night and 48 the next</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-3 mt-2">
      <li><strong class="text-white">Moved a half-metre.</strong> Corner placement and soft furnishings reshape reflections; measure from the same cushion each time.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white">Appliances cycled.</strong> A fridge, heater or street gust can lift Leq by 4 to 8 points for a minute, then vanish.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white">Hand versus table.</strong> Handling and nearby breathing inflate small capsules, so a table reading often drops several points with no change in the room.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-white">Mixed settings.</strong> Swapping A for C or Fast for Slow between nights breaks the comparison. Lock weighting and response first.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2 class="text-white font-semibold mt-6">Use level plus time before you react</h2>
    <p class="mt-2">Loudness alone rarely tells the story. Around 85 dBA across 8 hours marks the commonly cited screening boundary, and allowed time roughly halves with every 3 dB increase, while levels under 70 dBA seldom raise duration concern in everyday guidance. A 91 dBA mower patch matters differently from a 91 dBA second-long clatter, so pair any Leq with its duration. Browser figures stay screening-only; formal assessment belongs on purpose-built Class 1 or 2 instruments. To practise that pairing, run the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/background-noise-test/">background-noise test</a> and the <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/noise-exposure-calculator/">noise exposure calculator</a>, and read <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/noise-exposure/">noise exposure and hearing safety</a>.</p>
    <p class="mt-4"><a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/db-vs-dba/">dB vs dBA →</a> · <a class="underline underline-offset-4" href="/#measure">Open the meter →</a></p>
  </Article>
</Layout>
